From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dnode: do not move a dnode whose mutex is still held

dnode_move() decides a dnode is unreferenced by comparing its hold count
against the number of dbufs pointing at it. That misses a hold which is
on its way out but not gone: dnode_rele_and_unlock() removes the last
hold and only afterwards leaves dn_mtx, so in between the counts say the
dnode is free while a thread is still inside it. Relocating there leaves
the vacated copy with a held mutex, and mutex_destroy() on it fails
VERIFY0P(mutex_owner()), which on Linux ends in spl_panic() and a machine
that looks hung rather than crashed. Seen once in roughly ten runs of a
compaction workload over 200k files.

Take the owner of dn_mtx into account as well. Reading it without the
mutex is enough: to take it in order to add or drop a hold, a thread must
already hold a reference that is not one of the dbufs, which the count
has just ruled out, and the dbufs themselves cannot act because the
handle lock is held here.

This only matters where something calls dnode_move(), which until now was
nowhere on Linux.
---
diff --git a/module/zfs/dnode.c b/module/zfs/dnode.c
--- a/module/zfs/dnode.c
+++ b/module/zfs/dnode.c
@@ -1167,7 +1167,19 @@
 	DTRACE_PROBE3(dnode__move, dnode_t *, odn, int64_t, refcount,
 	    uint32_t, dbufs);
 
-	if (refcount > dbufs) {
+	/*
+	 * dn_mtx tells us about a hold that is on its way out but not gone yet.
+	 * dnode_rele_and_unlock() drops the last hold and only then leaves the
+	 * mutex, so between those two the dnode looks unreferenced while its
+	 * owner is still inside it; moving it there leaves the copy behind with
+	 * a held mutex, which the destructor refuses to take apart.
+	 *
+	 * Reading the owner without the mutex is enough here. Anyone who takes
+	 * it in order to add or drop a hold must already have a hold that is
+	 * not one of the dbufs, which is what the count above rules out, and
+	 * the dbufs themselves are shut out by the handle lock we hold.
+	 */
+	if (refcount > dbufs || mutex_owner(&odn->dn_mtx) != NULL) {
 		rw_exit(&odn->dn_struct_rwlock);
 		zrl_exit(&odn->dn_handle->dnh_zrlock);
 		mutex_exit(&os->os_lock);
